  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a coupled electrochemical–catalytic process that appears to generate hydrogen and oxygen while converting bromide and bromate between two linked cells. In the left electrolytic cell, a solution containing NaBr (and apparently sodium chromate) is split at two electrodes. At the cathode, water is reduced to release H2 and form OH−. The alkaline region near the cathode is associated with precipitation of Cr(OH)3, shown coating the electrode area. At the anode, bromide is oxidized to bromate, and electrons flow through the external circuit between the electrodes.\n\nA pump circulates the bromate-rich solution from the electrolytic cell into a separate catalytic cell on the right. There, in the presence of a solid catalyst, bromate seems to be converted back to bromide while O2 bubbles evolve from the liquid. The regenerated bromide is then returned to the electrolytic cell, creating a closed bromine oxyanion shuttle between the two vessels.\n\nOverall, the image shows a mediated water-splitting scheme: hydrogen is produced electrochemically in one chamber, oxygen is produced catalytically in another, and bromide/bromate serves as the recyclable redox mediator linking the two half-reactions.",
